-- Q516: Property Transactions: Basis [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 168(d)(1), the half-year convention treats all property as placed in service at the midpoint of the tax year regardless of actual date. For 7-year MACRS property using 200% declining balance, the full-year rate is 2/7 (~28.57%), but the half-year convention halves it to 14.29% in year one. Both Petrov and Santiago receive the identical 14.29% rate.
Wrong (A): The half-year convention ignores the specific month of acquisition, so March and November placements receive identical first-year rates.
Wrong (C): The half-year convention does reduce the first-year rate by half; it does not only affect the final year of the recovery period.
Wrong (D): The half-year convention does not pro-rate by quarter; that describes the mid-quarter convention under Section 168(d)(3), which applies only when more than 40% of property is placed in service in Q4.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 516;

-- Q520: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 1245, gain on disposition of personal property is recaptured as ordinary income to the extent of all depreciation previously allowed or allowable. The ordinary income portion equals the lesser of the gain realized or total accumulated depreciation. Any remaining gain above the depreciation amount is treated as Section 1231 gain.
Wrong (A): The gain up to accumulated depreciation is recaptured, not only the gain exceeding it; Section 1245 recaptures from the first dollar of gain.
Wrong (B): The 25% rate applies to unrecaptured Section 1250 gain on real property, not Section 1245 personal property recapture, which is taxed as ordinary income.
Wrong (C): Only gain up to accumulated depreciation is recaptured as ordinary income, not the entire gain regardless of depreciation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 520;

-- Q521: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 1(h)(1)(E), unrecaptured Section 1250 gain equals the straight-line depreciation claimed on real property, taxed at a maximum rate of 25%. The total gain is $400,000 ($800,000 minus $400,000 adjusted basis), of which $200,000 represents accumulated depreciation. That $200,000 is the unrecaptured Section 1250 gain.
Wrong (A): $400,000 is the total gain, not the unrecaptured Section 1250 portion, which is limited to the $200,000 of depreciation claimed.
Wrong (C): Unrecaptured Section 1250 gain is not $0; the $200,000 of straight-line depreciation taken on real property creates recapture taxed at up to 25%.
Wrong (D): $100,000 has no basis in the calculation; the full $200,000 of depreciation is the unrecaptured amount.$EXPL$
